## TitleText1('Linear Algebra with Applications')
## AuthorText1('David Lay')
## EditionText1('5')


########################################################################

DOCUMENT();      

loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "MathObjects.pl",
  "freemanMacros.pl",
  "answerHints.pl",
  "PGcourse.pl"
);

# Print problem number and point value (weight) for the problem
TEXT(beginproblem());

# Show which answers are correct and which ones are incorrect
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 1;

##############################################################
#
#  Setup
#
#
Context("Matrix");
$citytoburbs = Compute(random(21,30));
$burbstocity = Compute(random(15,20));

$transition = Matrix([[1-$citytoburbs/100,$burbstocity/100],[$citytoburbs/100,1-$burbstocity/100]]);

##############################################################
#
#  Text
#
#

Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_TEXT
$BR
In an urban area for a given year, approximately $citytoburbs% of people living in the city move to the suburbs 
(and the rest stay in the city) and $burbstocity% of people living in the suburbs move to the city (and the rest stay in the suburbs.)
$PAR
$BR

In this problem we explore a simplified model where nobody moves out of the area entirely and there are no births and no deaths.
$PAR
$BR

Find the transition matrix for this scenario. Assume the components of the state vector are listed in this order: [city population, suburb population]. 
The transition matrix should have the percentages written as decimals.
$PAR
$BR

\{$transition->ans_array\}
END_TEXT
Context()->normalStrings;

##############################################################
#
#  Answers
#
#
ANS($transition->cmp() );


ENDDOCUMENT();        